{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Moses was keeping.</b> Our master Moses took up this craft according to the custom of the early righteous ones, for so we find with Abel the righteous, and with the fathers of the world, and with the tribes. The reason was to distance himself from settled habitation, which is prone to sin, and furthermore in order to be secluded for prophecy. And what it says, 'after the wilderness' — Scripture teaches you that Moses would distance himself from the fields, so that he would not graze his flock in the field of others and come to theft. Scripture did not specify how many years Moses stayed in Midian. Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: forty years Moses spent in Egypt, and forty years in Midian, and forty years in the wilderness. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted concerning Rabbi Akiva, that his days were one hundred and twenty years like our master Moses, and they said of him: forty years he engaged in commerce, forty years he studied, forty years he taught. And likewise Hillel the Elder, also Rabban Yohanan ben Zakkai," "<b>And by way of</b> the plain sense (peshat): the matter of this passage is that Moses attained three things, and they are: the fire, the angel, and the Shekhinah. First he saw the fire that was blazing in the bush and the bush was not consumed, and he saw this with the physical eye literally while awake, for when he saw the bush burning in fire he recognized that it was fire, and he supposed it was sulphur fire from below; and when his mind was thus set and the bush was not being consumed, therefore he wanted to draw near. This is what it says: 'I will turn aside now and see this great sight,' that is, I will see this wonder — whether the bush has changed from other trees or the fire has changed from other fires; for had he supposed it was fire from above, he would not have drawn near. And after he saw this fire, his intellect was strengthened by the vision of the angel, and this is what it says: 'And an angel of YHWH appeared to him in a flame of fire from the midst of the bush' — the implication of Scripture is that he saw a flame of fire first, and afterward the angel from the midst of the fire. And after his intellect was strengthened by the vision of the angel, he saw in the vision of prophecy the glory of the Shekhinah, and this is what it says: 'And YHWH saw that he turned aside to see, and Elohim called to him.' And since this was the beginning of Moses' prophecy, the Holy One, blessed be He, wished to train him little by little and to elevate him from level to level until his intellect would be strengthened. A parable for the matter: a man who sits in a dark house for a long time — if he goes out suddenly and gazes at the sun, his sight will be darkened, and therefore he must look at the light little by little until he becomes accustomed to it. And just as this happens with the light of the sun, the same is the case and the same is the reason with the light of the intellect, for intellectual matters are like imaginary representations of natural things, for what happens to the intellect is like what happens to the senses, and the faculties of the soul are surely bound together with the faculties of the body," "<b>And so</b> we find with Israel, that the Holy One, blessed be He, trained them in the giving of the Torah little by little: first they were commanded at Marah with a few commandments, namely Sabbath and civil laws; and afterward they were commanded the Ten Commandments at Sinai; and afterward the completion of the Torah in the land of Moab. Therefore we find in the matter of apprehending Him, blessed be He, that a person needs to train his intellect little by little, and thereby his apprehension will ascend and expand steadily, like the matter of the light of dawn, which begins to shine with a little light and afterward goes and expands. And this is the language of the prophet (Hosea 6): 'ונדעה נרדפה לדעת את ה' כשחר נכון מוצאו' — Scripture teaches us that there are people who do not know how to pursue, and seek to attain knowledge all at once; and therefore it says 'ונדעה נרדפה לדעת' — let us first know how to pursue; and he explained the quality of the pursuit and said 'כשחר נכון מוצאו', that is, not all at once but little by little, like the dawn whose going forth is steady.'" "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: 'Now Moses was tending the flock of Jethro his father-in-law, the priest of Midian, and he led the flock behind the wilderness' — Moses saw the merit of Israel from the wilderness, as it says (Song 3): 'מי זאת עולה מן המדבר' — ascending from the wilderness; Torah from the wilderness; the Tabernacle from the wilderness; priesthood and kingship from the wilderness; the well from the wilderness; clouds of glory from the wilderness; therefore 'and he led the flock behind the wilderness.' Rabbi Shimon ben Lakish said: it is a halakhah — the flock of Moses went forty days and tasted nothing, just as Elijah went, as it says (I Kings 19): 'וילך בכח האכילה ההיא ארבעים יום וארבעים לילה.' 'And an angel of YHWH appeared to him in a flame of fire' — in order to flame his heart when they would come to Sinai and he would show them fires. And why from the midst of the bush, and not from a tree and not from a palm? The Holy One, blessed be He, said: I have written in My Torah 'עמו אנכי בצרה' — I am with him in his distress; My children are given into the iron crucible amid bondage, so I too reveal Myself from the midst of the bush, from a narrow place. That bush — that is Sinai; as it is written here 'והנה הסנה בוער באש', and it is written at Sinai (Deuteronomy 5): 'וההר בוער באש.'" "<b>And by way of</b> reason (sekhel): the matter of the bush alludes to the existence of lowly matter blazing in the fire of the supernal beings, and it would have been fitting for the matter to be consumed, since it and its fires are generated and corrupted. Therefore, upon seeing this sign, it was fitting to draw near in apprehension to ask why the bush was not consumed, and then the angel informed him that it is the fire of Elohim and a pure miracle. And from this the principle of faith in the creation of the world through the innovation of the miracle will be established. Or the bush alludes to the lowly people whose soul is entered by iron, and who are constantly blazing in the fire of troubles, and troubles encompass them from every side, and it would be fitting that they be consumed by their troubles — yet they stand and endure among the enemies by way of miracle and are not consumed. And this is (Deuteronomy 32): 'חצי אכלה בם' — a half consumed in them; they are consumed while they are not consumed. Or it alludes to Pharaoh, for thus the wicked are likened to thorns, as it is written (II Samuel 23): 'ובליעל כקוץ מונד כלהם', and he was blazing in the fire of the plagues and should have been consumed, yet he was not consumed, for the Holy One, blessed be He, was sustaining him through miracles in order to multiply His signs, in the manner of what is written (Exodus 9): 'ואולם בעבור זאת העמדתיך בעבור הראותך את כחי', and this interpretation is fitting according to the matter of the passage, for from the midst of the vision of the bush it was said to him: 'and now come and I will send you to Pharaoh.'" "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: 'and Moses was keeping' — he already was, for it did not say ויהי משה רועה as it said (Genesis 4): ״ויהי הבל רעה צאן״, nor did it say וירעה את צאן." "<b>And know</b> that it was prepared from the six days of creation to kill the Egyptian, and therefore it mentioned ״וירא מלאך ה׳ אליו בלבת אש.״ And you should already be roused by the letters of לכה. And like it, in my opinion, is (Job 1): ״איש היה בארץ עוץ איוב שמו״ — for it could have said ויהי איש בארץ עוץ איוב שמו, like (I Samuel 1): ״ויהי איש אחד מן הרמתים,״ (Ruth 1): ״ויהי בימי שפט השפטים,״ (Esther 1): ״ויהי בימי אחשורוש.״ And the fact that it mentioned a second time ״והיה האיש ההוא תם וישר,״ a הויה after a הויה, it appears that Job was in the third [level], for thus is the measure 'three times with a man' (Dan. 6), and from there he merited eternal life, that his soul would be bound in the bond of life at the end, after his earlier sins were atoned for him. And the fact that it mentioned אחר המדבר, its interpretation is that it includes an expression of governance, from the language of (Psalms 47): ״ידבר עמים תחתינו,״ and the intention is that from the midst of the flock he set his mind on the matter of providence — from what place does everything receive its sustenance — and he entered from matter to matter and from wisdom to wisdom until he arrived אל האלהים חורבה, that is, the mountain of Elohim, which is חורבה; it is the attribute of judgment, which is the second attribute to hesed, from whence comes providence to the world, as it is said (Psalms 136): ״נותן לחם לכל בשר כי לעולם חסדו.״ And it says 'the mountain of Elohim,' which is one of the patriarchs, and 'mountains' are nothing but patriarchs, as it is said (Micah 6): ״שמעו הרים את ריב ה׳,״ and it is from the language of הורים (parents). And it used the expression חורבה for the attribute of judgment, from the language of (Deuteronomy 33): ״ואשר חרב גאותך,״ which they explained by way of the Kabbalah that 'the pride of Israel' is the shoulder (אשור) which is the attribute of judgment. And from this our Rabbis of blessed memory derived: it was not called Mount חורב but rather Mount Sinai was its name; and why was it called חורב? Because from there destruction (חורבה) went forth for the idolaters. And so you find with Elijah (I Kings 19) in the matter of the cake of coals (עוגת רצפים) that he found at his head in the way of the wilderness, and it is written (there): ״וילך בכח האכילה ההיא ארבעים יום וארבעים לילה עד הר האלהים חורב.״ And our Rabbis of blessed memory understood the word אחר as in the sense of one who walks after his fellow, and from this they derived: every place where Moses walked in the wilderness, the wilderness would flee before him and become settled. And their intention in this is that the Holy One, blessed be He, would open for him gates of wisdom, and he would see that he had not yet reached what he sought until he came to the mountain of Elohim, which is חורבה, that is, the attribute of judgment; and it is the fire that consumes the offerings, as it is said (Leviticus 9): ״ותצא אש מלפני ה׳ ותאכל את העולה וגו׳,״ and corresponding to it in man is 'the soul that eats' (הנפש האוכלת) — for it is said (Leviticus 7): ״הנפש האוכלת״ — and therefore the angel appeared to him בלבת אש, as befitting the attribute of judgment, מתוך הסנה, and therefore the staff turned into a serpent, and his hand became leprous, and the waters turned to blood; and therefore at the end of the parashah it mentions (Exodus 4): ״ויחר אף ה׳ במשה,״ and written after it: ״ודבר הוא לך אל העם והיה הוא יהיה לך לפה,״ and in it the Name is hinted in reverse (למפרע) — since I have already informed you in the parashah of HaPalagah (Genesis 11) that it indicates the attribute of judgment everywhere that it is inscribed in reverse, and the subject matter of the parashah proves thus, as it is there." "<b>And behold,</b> this vision was a sign to Moses concerning what the Holy One, blessed be He, was destined to give the Torah in fire through him in this place, and all Israel would hear ״אנכי״ and ״לא יהיה לך״ from the mouth of the Omnipotent." "<b>And the opinion</b> of the Ramban, of blessed memory, in this parashah is that what it says ״וירא מלאך ה׳ אליו בלבת אש״ and what it says ״ויקרא אליו אלהים״ are all one; and this is what it says ״מלאך ה׳ מתוך הסנה״ and it says ״אלהים מתוך הסנה.״ And so you find at the Sea, where it is written (Exodus 14): ״ויסע מלאך האלהים,״ and it is illuminated that they are not adjacent. And do not wonder how Moses would hide his face from the angel, for the angel mentioned here is the redeeming angel, about whom it is written (Genesis 31): ״אנכי האל בית אל,״ and likewise it says here: ״אנכי אלהי אביך אלהי אברהם אלהי יצחק ואלהי יעקב,״ and it is about him that it is said (Exodus 23): ״כי שמי בקרבו.״"], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:56:12.544599114Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}